/* First-reading lessons. The original technical material remains available as optional depth. */
const simpleLessons = {
tensors: {
 title:'Why tokens become numbers',
 subtitle:'Start with a table of numbers. Then see why a model needs it.',
 goals:['Explain why one token needs many numbers','Read the shape of a small table','Calculate one small transformation by hand'],
 body:
 h('Why does a token need more than one number?',p('A <strong>token</strong> is a piece of text the model processes. It might be a word, part of a word, or punctuation. The model gives each token an ID, like a number on a library book. That identifies the token, but the model also needs a representation it can calculate with.')+p('Think about describing a car. Its ID tells you which car it is. But comparing cars takes several numbers: weight, speed, price, and fuel use. In a similar way, a model represents a token using a list of numbers. That list is called a <strong>vector</strong>.')+code('Token ID:       427\n                   ↓ look up a learned table\nInitial vector: [0.2, -0.7, 0.4, ...]', 'picture of the idea')+p('The car comparison has a limit: a token’s coordinates usually do not have simple labels such as “noun” or “happiness.” The model learns how to use the numbers together. Information is spread across them.'))+
 h('The same token can mean different things',p('Compare “She deposited money at the <strong>bank</strong>” with “She sat on the river <strong>bank</strong>.” Assuming “bank” is the same token, it starts with the same initial vector in both sentences. Later layers use the surrounding words to change that vector. The two final representations can therefore be different.')+flow(['Token ID','Initial numbers','Use surrounding text','Updated numbers'])+p('More coordinates give the model more room to represent useful distinctions. They also require more memory and computation. In our Muse expert problem, each token arrives as <strong>4,096 numbers</strong>. That is one token with 4,096 coordinates, not 4,096 tokens.'))+
 h('What is a tensor? What is its shape?',p('A <strong>tensor</strong> is a container of numbers. For now, imagine a table. Put one token’s vector in each row:')+tbl(['','Feature 1','Feature 2','Feature 3'],[['Token A','0.2','0.8','0.1'],['Token B','0.5','0.3','0.9']])+p('This table has 2 rows and 3 columns, so its <strong>shape is [2, 3]</strong>. Shape tells you how the numbers are organized. A single number is a scalar, a list is a vector, and a two-dimensional table is a matrix. All of these are tensors. “Feature” here simply means a coordinate in the representation.'))+
 h('How do weights change the numbers?',p('<strong>Weights</strong> are numbers learned during training. Once we are serving the trained model, those weights stay fixed while the token representations change.')+code('Token:    [2, 3]\nWeights:  [1, 4]\n\nMultiply matching numbers, then add:\n(2 × 1) + (3 × 4) = 14\n\nAnother weight row: [5, 6]\n(2 × 5) + (3 × 6) = 28\n\nToken before: [2, 3]\nToken after:  [14, 28]', 'worked example')+p('Doing this for many tokens and many weight rows is <strong>matrix multiplication</strong>. The @ symbol in Python means matrix multiplication. You do not need to memorize the notation yet; understand the multiply-and-add example first.'))+
 note('Keep these three ideas','A tensor holds numbers. Its shape describes their organization. Learned weights transform a token’s numbers into new numbers.')+
 exercise('Three tokens each have four numbers. What is the table’s shape?',p('<strong>[3, 4]</strong>: three rows for the tokens, four columns for each token’s coordinates. There are 3 × 4 = 12 numbers in total.')),
 quiz:[{q:'In our Muse expert input, 4,096 numbers describe…',options:['One token’s current representation','4,096 separate tokens'],answer:0,why:'Each token is represented by a vector with 4,096 coordinates.'},{q:'A table has 2 token rows and 3 features per row. Its shape is…',options:['[2, 3]','[3, 2]'],answer:0,why:'For this table we write [rows, columns]: [2, 3].'}]
},
pytorch:{
 title:'How Python asks the GPU to work',
 subtitle:'Your code gives instructions. The GPU carries out the large parallel calculations.',
 goals:['Tell the CPU and GPU jobs apart','Explain why copying numbers takes time','Understand why finishing a Python line does not mean the GPU has finished'],
 body:h('Why do we use PyTorch?',p('Imagine writing every multiply and add in the previous chapter by hand. A real model might transform thousands of numbers for many tokens, repeatedly. <strong>PyTorch</strong> is a Python library that lets us describe those operations compactly.')+code('x = torch.tensor([[2., 3.]])\nw = torch.tensor([[1., 4.], [5., 6.]])\ny = x @ w.T   # produces [[14., 28.]]', 'small PyTorch example')+p('Here x holds the token’s numbers. w holds two weight rows. <code>w.T</code> turns the weight table so matrix multiplication can use it. This is the same calculation we already did by hand. The full runnable setup is in “Go deeper.”'))+
 h('Who gives the instruction? Who does the arithmetic?',p('The <strong>CPU</strong> runs your Python program and coordinates the work. The <strong>GPU</strong> is built to do many similar calculations at once. A <strong>kernel</strong> is a function that runs on the GPU.')+flow(['Python on CPU','Queue a GPU operation','GPU calculates','Result stays on GPU'])+p('Think of the CPU as the person organizing orders and the GPU as a large team preparing them. The analogy is about coordination, not intelligence: both are processors, and both can do arithmetic.')+p('A tensor also has a <strong>device</strong>: where its numbers live. A CPU tensor lives in CPU-accessible memory; a CUDA tensor is on an NVIDIA GPU. CUDA is NVIDIA’s GPU programming platform. Operations normally need their inputs on compatible devices.'))+
 h('Why moving and copying numbers matters',p('Suppose a large table is already on the GPU. Sending it back to the CPU after every operation and then returning it to the GPU would waste time. Usually we keep intermediate results on the GPU until we need the final output.')+p('A <strong>view</strong> is another way to look at existing storage, like looking at the same spreadsheet from a different angle. A <strong>copy</strong> creates another set of numbers. Copies consume time and space. Also, allocating space does not always fill it: <code>torch.empty</code> gives you storage whose values must be written before you use them.'))+
 h('Why a quick Python line may hide slow GPU work',p('The CPU can hand an order to the GPU and continue before the order is finished. This is called <strong>asynchronous execution</strong>. If you time only the Python line, you might measure how long handing over the order took.')+code('CPU:  send the order ── continue running\nGPU:        start calculation ───────── finish', 'timing picture')+p('A <strong>synchronization</strong> makes the CPU wait for the relevant GPU work. That helps when measuring elapsed time, but waiting after every tiny operation can make the program slower. Later we will use proper timing tools rather than guessing from Python’s speed.'))+
 h('How this maps to our competition',p('<code>prepare(weights)</code> is setup: arrange fixed weights and compile code once. <code>kernel_fn(data, state)</code> is the repeated job: process the current tokens. It is like preparing the kitchen before service, then cooking each new order. You cannot prepare today’s answer using yesterday’s tokens.'))+
 note('The main idea','Keep the numbers near the GPU work, avoid unnecessary copies, and measure when the work actually finishes.')+
 exercise('Python returns from a GPU operation almost immediately. Is the calculation necessarily finished?',p('<strong>No.</strong> It may only have been queued. A correct measurement must account for the GPU completing the calculation.')),
 quiz:[{q:'What usually runs your Python program?',options:['The CPU','The GPU only'],answer:0,why:'The CPU runs Python and dispatches GPU operations through libraries such as PyTorch.'},{q:'What can torch.empty safely promise?',options:['Space to store values','A table already full of zeros'],answer:0,why:'The values are uninitialized. Write them before reading them.'}]
},
transformers:{
 title:'How a model uses the surrounding words',
 subtitle:'Follow one token as it reads context, changes its numbers, and helps predict what comes next.',
 goals:['Explain attention using a sentence','Understand the roles of Q, K, and V','Describe a model layer without needing its equations'],
 body:h('Why do token numbers need to change?',p('In “river bank,” the word “river” helps determine what “bank” means. In “deposit money at the bank,” different surrounding words matter. A useful model therefore needs a way to move information from one token’s representation into another.')+p('<strong>Attention</strong> is the operation that does this. For a token, it gives different earlier positions different importance, then mixes information from those positions. In a model generating text from left to right, it cannot read future words that have not been generated.'))+
 h('Q, K, and V are three versions of the token’s numbers',p('The model uses learned weights to create three vectors from a token’s current representation:')+tbl(['Name','A useful way to think about it'],[['Query (Q)','What information is this position looking for?'],['Key (K)','How can another position match this one?'],['Value (V)','What information can this position contribute?']])+p('These are numerical vectors, not sentences or literal questions. The model compares a query with available keys to produce scores. It turns the scores into weights, and uses those weights to mix the values.')+p('These attention weights are calculated from the current inputs. They are different from the fixed, learned model weights in chapter 1. The score-to-weight step is called <strong>softmax</strong>. Its weights are positive and add up to one. A <strong>causal mask</strong> prevents future positions from participating.'))+
 h('A tiny attention example',p('Imagine that two earlier positions offer single-number values 2 and 10. After comparing keys, attention assigns them weights 0.75 and 0.25:')+code('Contribution from position A: 0.75 × 2  = 1.5\nContribution from position B: 0.25 × 10 = 2.5\n\nCombined information: 1.5 + 2.5 = 4', 'worked example')+p('Real values are vectors, so this weighted combination happens for many coordinates. Multiple <strong>heads</strong> let the model make several different learned comparisons. Position information also matters; one method, <strong>RoPE</strong>, changes query and key coordinates according to token position. You can learn its rotation equations later.'))+
 h('What happens after information is mixed?',p('Another part of the layer transforms each token’s own features. It is called an <strong>MLP</strong>, short for multilayer perceptron. Think of attention as gathering relevant information and the MLP as processing the resulting representation. Both are learned numerical operations.')+p('Two common supporting operations are a <strong>residual addition</strong>, which adds the old representation to the new contribution, and <strong>normalization</strong>, which rescales numbers based on their magnitude. The exact order matters. Muse also uses gated MLPs: one calculated vector modulates another. The name <strong>SwiGLU</strong> identifies a particular version of that operation.'))+
 h('How does this become a new word?',flow(['Token numbers','Read context with attention','Transform features','Repeat through layers','Score possible next tokens'])+p('The final numbers are converted into scores for vocabulary tokens. Those scores are called <strong>logits</strong>. A selection procedure chooses the next token. It might take the highest score or sample according to a probability distribution. That token is appended, and the process continues.')+note('One important distinction','The attention weights in this chapter decide how to mix token positions. The expert routing weights in chapter 5 decide how to mix expert outputs. They are different uses of weighted sums.'))+
 exercise('If attention gives a value of 4 weight 0.5, and a value of 8 weight 0.5, what is the result?',p('0.5 × 4 + 0.5 × 8 = 2 + 4 = <strong>6</strong>. Each weight determines how much that value contributes.')),
 quiz:[{q:'What helps “bank” have a different representation in different sentences?',options:['Using information from surrounding tokens','Changing its vocabulary ID every time'],answer:0,why:'The initial token can be the same while later hidden representations depend on the context.'},{q:'What does attention combine using its weights?',options:['Values (V)','The names of the tokens'],answer:0,why:'It combines numerical value vectors from the allowed positions.'}]
},
'kv-cache':{
 title:'Why generation has two phases',
 subtitle:'Read the prompt once. Then continue the sentence without starting from scratch.',
 goals:['Tell prefill and decode apart','Explain what the KV cache remembers','Understand why more context needs more memory'],
 body:h('First read, then continue',p('Suppose the prompt is “The cat”. The model first processes the prompt and produces scores for the next token. It might choose “sat”. Processing the prompt is called <strong>prefill</strong>.')+p('Next, the model processes the newly chosen “sat”, uses the previous context, and predicts another token. It might choose “down”. Continuing this way is called <strong>decode</strong>. In ordinary generation, each chosen token affects the next prediction.')+code('Prompt:          The cat\nPrefill:         process the prompt → choose "sat"\nDecode step 1:   process "sat"       → choose "down"\nDecode step 2:   process "down"      → choose the next token', 'simplified example · words shown as tokens')+p('Actual tokenization may split words differently. The example shows the order of work, not a guaranteed model response.'))+
 h('Why remember the earlier work?',p('When predicting after “The cat sat”, the model still needs information about “The” and “cat”. Recomputing every previous token at every step would be expensive. Instead, it stores the previous <strong>keys and values</strong> from attention. This stored information is the <strong>KV cache</strong>.')+p('Think of it as keeping useful notes from the pages you already read. You still consult the notes when you continue, but you do not need to recreate them every time. In causal attention with fixed weights, adding a later token does not change the earlier positions’ keys and values.')+flow(['New token','Create its Q, K, V','Consult saved K/V','Save its new K/V']))+
 h('The cache saves computation, but costs memory',p('Use a made-up model where one saved token requires 1 KiB, or 1,024 bytes, of cache. Saving 1,000 tokens takes 1,000 KiB. Saving 2,000 takes twice that. If ten separate requests each store 1,000 tokens, the total is ten times one request’s cache.')+p('Real models store keys and values in many layers and heads. The exact size depends on those dimensions and the number of bytes used per value. The interactive lab shows a more realistic calculation; its settings are a hypothetical model, not Muse’s exact configuration.'))+
 h('Why long prompts can still slow decode',p('Keeping notes does not make reading them free. Full attention still reads the relevant cached history for each new token. A longer context can therefore take more memory and more work per generated token, even with a KV cache.')+p('A <strong>prefix cache</strong> can share previously computed work when requests start with the same compatible text. A <strong>paged cache</strong> stores history in manageable blocks rather than requiring one large continuous region. These solve different problems: reuse and allocation.'))+
 h('Requests and token rows are different counts',p('One ordinary decode step for 16 active requests usually processes 16 new token rows. But if each request proposes eight future tokens for verification, the target processes 16 × 8 = <strong>128 token rows</strong>. That is why a kernel can see a much larger batch than the request count suggests.')+note('Keep the central idea','The KV cache avoids recalculating earlier keys and values. It does not remove the memory needed to store them or the cost of reading them.'))+
 exercise('A made-up cache uses 1 KiB per token. Four requests each hold 2,000 tokens. How much cache is needed?',p('4 × 2,000 × 1 KiB = <strong>8,000 KiB</strong>, about 7.81 MiB. This assumes separate histories with no sharing or extra allocation overhead.')),
 quiz:[{q:'Which phase first processes the input prompt?',options:['Prefill','Decode'],answer:0,why:'Prefill processes the prompt. Decode continues from the generated tokens.'},{q:'What is remembered in the KV cache?',options:['Earlier attention keys and values','Every possible future answer'],answer:0,why:'The cache stores already computed attention state. It does not know the future tokens.'}]
},
moe:{
 title:'Why a model chooses experts',
 subtitle:'Send each token to a few selected networks, then combine their answers.',
 goals:['Explain what an expert and a router do','Follow a two-expert weighted sum','See why doing unused work wastes GPU time'],
 body:h('Why have several experts?',p('An <strong>expert</strong> is a small neural network inside the larger model. A <strong>mixture of experts</strong>, or MoE, contains many such networks. For each token, a <strong>router</strong> chooses a few experts to run. The model can therefore have many expert parameters without using every expert for every token.')+p('Imagine an office with many specialists. A coordinator sends one task to a few people and combines their contributions. This is only an analogy: learned experts do not necessarily correspond to clear human subjects such as “math” or “grammar.”'))+
 h('Follow one token through two experts',p('Suppose the router chooses expert 0 and expert 2. After they process the token, their output vectors are [2, 4] and [6, 8]. The router gives them weights 0.75 and 0.25.')+code('Expert 0 contribution: 0.75 × [2, 4] = [1.5, 3]\nExpert 2 contribution: 0.25 × [6, 8] = [1.5, 2]\n\nCombined result: [1.5, 3] + [1.5, 2] = [3, 5]', 'toy combination · assumes outputs are ready to combine')+p('A weight of 0.75 means the output is multiplied by 0.75. It does not mean “75% of the token.” In the Muse task, each expert’s output must also be normalized before this weighted combination. The example above starts after that step.'))+
 h('Why group tokens that chose the same expert?',p('Suppose tokens A, B, and C all choose expert 2. That expert can process their rows together and reuse the same weights. If we process each token separately, we may reload those weights repeatedly. Grouping work can improve reuse, but arranging the groups also has a cost.')+p('The slow starter did something much more wasteful: it ran all local experts on every token, including experts that were not selected. It then gave unused results zero weight. Throwing away an answer does not recover the time spent calculating it.'))+
 h('The real Muse numbers',tbl(['Thing','Meaning in our expert problem'],[['256 experts','The total set available to the router'],['Top-8','Each token selects eight experts'],['32 local experts','The experts owned by the GPU being tested'],['4,096 features','Numbers in each token’s expert input'],['Zero local routes','This GPU contributes a zero vector for that token']])+p('Different GPUs own different experts. A token may choose some experts here and others elsewhere. The GPU in our kernel problem computes only its share. The full system combines the shares.'))+
 h('Not every expert gets the same amount of work',p('If many tokens choose one expert, that expert becomes busy while others may have little to do. This is called <strong>routing imbalance</strong>. Equal numbers of experts per GPU do not guarantee equal computation. A step that needs all GPU contributions can wait for the busiest one.')+note('The optimization goal','Skip unselected work, reuse selected weights efficiently, and preserve the required order: expert computation, per-expert normalization, then weighted combination.'))+
 exercise('A token selects experts 2 and 7. Should we calculate expert 3’s output and multiply it by zero?',p('No need. If expert 3 was not selected, its contribution is zero. Avoiding its calculation saves work. We must still produce a correctly initialized output and safely handle all selected experts.')),
 quiz:[{q:'What does the router choose?',options:['Which experts process each token','Which words the tokenizer recognizes'],answer:0,why:'Routing selects the expert networks and their combination weights.'},{q:'Why can the slow starter be correct but slow?',options:['It computes outputs that it later throws away','Correct answers must always be slow'],answer:0,why:'Unselected experts still consume time if we calculate them before multiplying their contributions by zero.'}]
},
gpu:{
 title:'How a GPU divides the work',
 subtitle:'Many workers help only when they can get the right data and coordinate correctly.',
 goals:['Understand threads, warps, and blocks','Explain why the location of data matters','Recognize why adding more workers is not always faster'],
 body:h('Why use a GPU for these calculations?',p('If you have a million independent pairs of numbers to add, many additions can happen at once. A GPU has hardware designed to keep many similar operations in progress. A <strong>kernel</strong> tells those workers what to calculate and which numbers to use.')+p('More parallel work is not automatically faster. The workers need data, enough useful tasks, and a way to coordinate. A small job may spend more time getting started than doing arithmetic.'))+
 h('Give the worker groups their names',p('A <strong>thread</strong> is one execution of the kernel’s instructions for its assigned work. On NVIDIA GPUs, threads run in groups of 32 called <strong>warps</strong>. Several warps can belong to a <strong>thread block</strong>. Blocks run on hardware units called <strong>streaming multiprocessors</strong>, or SMs.')+flow(['GPU','Many SMs','Blocks on each SM','Warps of 32 threads'])+p('Think of a workshop with rooms, teams, and workers. The comparison helps describe grouping; it does not imply every worker independently executes every possible instruction at the same time. A block’s threads can cooperate using shared storage and synchronization.'))+
 h('A desk is faster to reach than a warehouse',tbl(['GPU term','Useful analogy','What to remember'],[['Registers','Numbers on a worker’s desk','Fast, small, usually private to a thread'],['Shared memory','A team’s workbench','Threads in a block cooperate using it'],['L2 cache','A shared nearby supply area','Hardware keeps some recently useful data here'],['Device memory','The large warehouse','Holds weights and tensors; moving data takes time']])+p('Good kernels reuse numbers close to the work. If every multiplication fetches the same weight from the warehouse again, the workers may spend much of their time waiting.')+p('A <strong>tile</strong> is a manageable piece of a big calculation. Bring in a small piece of each input, use it for several outputs, and then move to the next piece.'))+
 h('Why more workers can make things worse',p('Each resident block needs some registers and shared memory. If one block uses a huge amount, fewer blocks fit at once. If a thread needs too many registers, some values may be moved into slower memory. That is called a <strong>register spill</strong>.')+p('<strong>Occupancy</strong> describes how many warps are resident compared with what the SM can support. Higher occupancy can hide waiting, but a kernel with more reuse and lower occupancy may still win. We need measurements, not a rule that “more is always better.”'))+
 h('Workers must know when shared results are ready',p('If worker A writes a value that worker B needs, B must not read it too early. <strong>Synchronization</strong> establishes the required ordering. A barrier for one block does not automatically wait for all blocks in the GPU.')+p('<strong>Tensor cores</strong> are specialized hardware for supported matrix operations. Their speed helps only when the computation is arranged in a compatible way. They do not make every operation or every number format equally fast.')+note('The main idea','GPU optimization is about useful parallel work, data reuse, and correct coordination—not just making more threads.'))+
 exercise('A bigger tile reuses more data but uses almost all shared memory. Could it lose to a smaller tile?',p('<strong>Yes.</strong> Fewer blocks may fit on the SM, reducing the ability to hide waits. The extra reuse must be worth that resource cost.')),
 quiz:[{q:'An NVIDIA warp contains how many threads?',options:['32','4','1,024'],answer:0,why:'Thirty-two threads form a warp. Multiple warps can belong to one block.'},{q:'Why reuse a weight after loading it?',options:['To avoid paying for the same data movement repeatedly','To change the model’s learned value'],answer:0,why:'Reuse saves memory traffic while keeping the same arithmetic values.'}]
},
performance:{
 title:'Find what is making the work slow',
 subtitle:'A fast calculator can still spend most of its time waiting for numbers.',
 goals:['Separate time spent moving data from time spent calculating','Explain why batch size changes performance','Estimate the benefit of improving only one component'],
 body:h('Start with a factory, not a formula',p('Imagine a factory that needs 5 minutes to receive materials and 20 minutes to process them. If deliveries and processing overlap perfectly across the work, calculation is the larger limit. Buying faster trucks will not remove the 20-minute processing requirement.')+p('A GPU has a similar tradeoff. <strong>Bandwidth</strong> describes how much data can be moved per second. <strong>Compute throughput</strong> describes how much arithmetic can be done per second. The slower part of that combination can limit performance. Real dependencies may also prevent perfect overlap.'))+
 h('Now attach small numbers',p('Suppose an operation must read 24 MB, and the assumed memory system can deliver 4.8 MB per microsecond. Moving those bytes needs at least 24 ÷ 4.8 = <strong>5 microseconds</strong>. A microsecond, written µs, is one millionth of a second.')+p('If its arithmetic needs at least 20 µs, the idealized lower bound is the larger number: 20 µs. If the arithmetic needs only 2 µs, memory gives the larger bound: 5 µs. Setup, waiting, and imperfect use of hardware can make actual time longer.')+note('The formula after the idea','Optimistic minimum time ≈ the larger of “bytes ÷ bandwidth” and “arithmetic work ÷ compute throughput.” It is a starting estimate, not a promise.'))+
 h('Why more tokens can help',p('Imagine reading a recipe once and using it to prepare 16 servings. That can be more efficient than retrieving the recipe separately for each serving. Similarly, a batch of token rows may reuse the same matrix weights.')+p('Multiplying one token vector by a matrix is often called <strong>GEMV</strong>. Multiplying a table of token rows by a matrix is <strong>GEMM</strong>. More rows can create more opportunities for reuse. They also increase total work, so larger batches are not automatically lower-latency.'))+
 h('Why combine several operations?',p('Suppose separate GPU functions calculate a scale, write a temporary table, read it back to normalize it, then write and read it again to combine outputs. <strong>Fusion</strong> puts operations together so some temporary results can stay close to the workers. It can also reduce the number of launches—the requests to start GPU work.')+p('But combining everything can use too much fast storage or require more coordination. Our first custom matrix kernels lost to the existing optimized library. “Custom” and “fused” are implementation choices, not evidence of speed.'))+
 h('Twice as fast in one part is not twice as fast overall',code('Original step:\n  MoE experts:  50 ms\n  Everything else: 50 ms\n  Total:       100 ms\n\nMake the experts 2× faster:\n  MoE experts:  25 ms\n  Everything else: 50 ms\n  Total:        75 ms\n\nWhole-step speedup = 100 / 75 = 1.33×', 'worked example · illustrative times')+p('This relationship is called <strong>Amdahl’s law</strong>. It helps us choose where to spend effort. A major improvement to a tiny part may barely change the complete system.'))+
 exercise('A step takes 100 ms, and only 10 ms is the kernel you change. You reduce that kernel to 1 ms. What is the new total?',p('The unchanged part is 90 ms. Add the improved 1 ms: <strong>91 ms total</strong>. That is about 1.10× overall speedup, even though the kernel itself became 10× faster.')),
 quiz:[{q:'If moving data is the main limit, will faster arithmetic necessarily help much?',options:['No','Yes, always'],answer:0,why:'The operation can still wait for the same data. Address the measured bottleneck.'},{q:'Half of a step becomes twice as fast. What is the total speedup?',options:['1.33×','2×'],answer:0,why:'100 units of time become 25 + 50 = 75. The speedup is 100/75.'}]
},
precision:{
 title:'Store numbers with fewer bits',
 subtitle:'Smaller representations save space, but you must understand what changes and what stays exact.',
 goals:['Explain the difference between a value and its encoding','Distinguish rounding from rearranging stored data','Know why a fast answer still needs correctness checks'],
 body:h('Why not keep every number as accurately as possible?',p('Imagine writing 3.14159265 versus 3.14. The shorter form takes less space, but it loses information. Computers face a related tradeoff: each number has a limited number of <strong>bits</strong>, the binary digits used to store it. Eight bits make one byte.')+p('A 32-bit format uses four bytes per value. A 16-bit format uses two. That matters when a model has billions of values to store and move. Smaller storage can save memory traffic; conversion and arithmetic costs still matter too.'))+
 h('What do FP32 and BF16 mean?',p('<strong>FP32</strong> is a 32-bit floating-point format. “Floating point” means numbers can represent different scales using an exponent, somewhat like scientific notation. <strong>BF16</strong> is a particular 16-bit format. FP16 is another 16-bit format, with a different balance of range and precision.')+p('Do not treat all formats with the same bit count as interchangeable. <strong>Range</strong> is how large or small a magnitude can be. <strong>Precision</strong> concerns how finely values can be distinguished. A format can have wide range and coarse spacing.')+p('Also, storing inputs in BF16 does not require adding all intermediate products in BF16. A kernel can keep a running sum in FP32, then round its output to BF16. The running-sum format is the <strong>accumulation precision</strong>.'))+
 h('A small code can stand for a scaled number',p('Muse’s expert weights use a four-bit representation called <strong>NVFP4</strong>. Four bits allow only 16 code patterns, so scales help represent useful values. For this task, a decoded code value is multiplied by a small-group scale and an expert-projection scale.')+code('Decoded code value: 3\nSmall-group scale:  0.5\nGlobal scale:       2\n\nActual weight = 3 × 0.5 × 2 = 3', 'worked example')+p('Two four-bit codes fit in one byte. Extracting those codes and applying their scales is <strong>dequantization</strong>. A kernel can decode a small piece while using it instead of permanently expanding every stored weight.'))+
 h('Changing a number is different from moving it',p('<strong>Quantization</strong> chooses a restricted representation for values, usually with rounding. <strong>Repacking</strong> changes how existing values or codes are arranged. Think of putting the same books in a different shelf order so they are easier to retrieve. Their text stays the same.')+p('Our challenge allows lossless repacking but does not allow permanently replacing packed expert weights with expanded BF16 weights. “Lossless” means the original represented values can be recovered exactly. The public kernel’s layout optimization works on that basis.'))+
 h('Why compare with a trusted answer?',p('Changing the order of additions can change rounding. A memory bug can spoil only one row. An invalid value called <strong>NaN</strong> can spread through a result; even NaN multiplied by zero is still NaN. That is why “it ran without crashing” is not enough.')+p('We compare with a straightforward reference calculation, check every required shape, and examine error limits. The allowed error depends on the task. Passing one test does not authorize changing the required precision.'))+
 exercise('You rearrange stored four-bit codes and their matching scales without changing any values. Is that quantization or repacking?',p('<strong>Repacking.</strong> You changed where the information lives, not which values it represents.')),
 quiz:[{q:'What is lossless repacking?',options:['Rearranging the same represented values','Rounding values to a different format'],answer:0,why:'A lossless layout change preserves the values and changes their arrangement.'},{q:'Can a BF16 input use an FP32 running sum?',options:['Yes','No'],answer:0,why:'Storage and accumulation formats are distinct, subject to the required computation rules.'}]
},
kernels:{
 title:'Write one small GPU job',
 subtitle:'Before a large expert kernel, understand how to add two short lists safely.',
 goals:['Explain what work each GPU worker owns','Understand why the last group needs a boundary check','Choose a manageable sequence of kernel exercises'],
 body:h('Start with a job you can check in your head',p('We want to add two lists element by element:')+code('A = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nB = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\n\nC = [11, 22, 33, 44, 55]', 'our first GPU job')+p('Every output position is independent. The worker for position 0 reads A[0] and B[0], adds them, and writes C[0]. Another worker can handle position 1. The arithmetic is easy; assigning the right positions safely is the key.'))+
 h('What if our groups do not fit perfectly?',p('Pretend each group handles four positions. The first group handles positions 0–3. The second group is assigned 4–7, but our lists contain only five entries. Position 4 is valid; positions 5, 6, and 7 do not exist.')+code('Group 0: positions 0  1  2  3  → all valid\nGroup 1: positions 4  5  6  7  → only 4 is valid\n\nFor each assigned position i:\n    if i < number_of_elements:\n        C[i] = A[i] + B[i]', 'pseudocode · explains the algorithm')+p('This validity condition is called a <strong>mask</strong>. It must protect reads as well as writes. Accessing beyond the allocation is a bug even if the program happens not to crash immediately.'))+
 h('Where Triton helps',p('<strong>Triton</strong> is a language and compiler for writing GPU kernels using Python-like code. You describe blocks of work and their memory accesses. The compiler turns that into instructions for supported GPUs. A Triton program instance is not simply one Python loop iteration or one GPU thread; it describes a block of work.')+p('Our first kernel can use this plan: calculate the group’s positions, mask positions outside the list, load the two inputs, add them, and store the outputs. The full executable code is in the optional technical section. Start with the plan before the syntax.'))+
 h('Build up in small steps',tbl(['Exercise','What it teaches'],[['Add two vectors','Assign indices and handle the last partial group'],['Sum one row','Make several values contribute to one answer'],['Normalize a row','Reuse the sum to rescale the row'],['Combine two operations','Avoid writing an unnecessary intermediate'],['Multiply small matrices','Reuse tiles of values across outputs']])+p('A sum over a row is a <strong>reduction</strong>: many inputs become one value. Matrix multiplication introduces <strong>tiling</strong>: working on small pieces that reuse loaded data. Each exercise introduces a new difficulty without requiring you to understand a whole model first.'))+
 h('When should you read the big CUDA kernel?',p('Once small kernels make sense, <strong>CUDA C++</strong> exposes more direct control over threads, storage, and synchronization. The public MoE kernel uses advanced ways to overlap data loading with arithmetic. Those details are easier to learn when you can point to the work they save.')+note('Running the examples','The website’s labs work in your browser. Actual NVIDIA GPU kernels need a compatible GPU environment. Our Mac can edit the code, but it cannot run CUDA kernels locally. H200-specific timings need the appropriate hardware.'))+
 exercise('Our list has five values and a group handles four. How many groups are needed?',p('<strong>Two groups.</strong> One covers positions 0–3; the second covers position 4 and masks off its three invalid positions.')),
 quiz:[{q:'Why do we mask the last partial group?',options:['To avoid accessing elements that do not exist','To make all values zero'],answer:0,why:'The launch may cover more positions than the list contains. Invalid positions must not read or write memory.'},{q:'What should the first kernel do?',options:['A small operation with an easily checked answer','The entire model at once'],answer:0,why:'A small example lets us understand indexing and correctness before adding complexity.'}]
},
profiling:{
 title:'Know whether a change really helped',
 subtitle:'Measure the same job fairly, then explain where its time went.',
 goals:['Define a fair before-and-after comparison','Understand warmup and repeated measurements','Explain the difference between a benchmark and a profile'],
 body:h('First decide what you are comparing',p('Suppose runner A completes 100 metres in 12 seconds and runner B completes 80 metres in 10 seconds. Comparing only the seconds would be misleading. GPU measurements also need matching conditions: the same required work, input sizes, precision, and hardware.')+p('Write a question such as: “Does combining these normalization operations save time for 16 token rows?” That gives the experiment a specific purpose. Save the original version so you can compare or return to it.'))+
 h('Separate opening the shop from serving an order',p('The first run may compile code, allocate storage, or initialize a library. If we care about repeated serving speed, those startup costs should be measured separately. <strong>Warmup</strong> means running the operation enough to complete that setup before measuring steady work.')+p('We must also wait for the GPU work to finish, as chapter 2 explained. Then repeat the measurement: a busy machine, changing clock rate, or different cache contents can affect a single result.'))+
 h('A benchmark and a profile answer different questions',p('A <strong>benchmark</strong> measures how long the defined job takes. A <strong>profile</strong> breaks down where time or resources are spent. Think of timing a restaurant order versus watching how much time it spends waiting, cooking, and being delivered.')+tbl(['Question','Useful observation'],[['Did this version get faster?','Matched benchmark times'],['Which operations dominate?','An operation or GPU timeline'],['Is a kernel waiting for memory?','Detailed hardware measurements'],['Does the whole server improve?','End-to-end request measurements']])+p('Profiling itself can add overhead. Use it to understand behavior, then use a controlled benchmark to judge the improvement.'))+
 h('Why our challenge uses a particular measurement style',p('A <strong>CUDA graph</strong> records a sequence of GPU operations for efficient replay. It reduces repeated CPU dispatch work. A <strong>cold cache</strong> test starts without relying on the needed values already being in the GPU’s fast cache. Both choices affect which optimizations help.')+p('Our competition check uses graph replays and cold L2 cache. Measuring only ordinary Python calls with recently used data could give us a winner that loses on the actual judge.'))+
 h('Read the result without overclaiming',p('Our earlier tuned version scored <strong>1.263×</strong> against the SGLang baseline. The public CUDA candidate checked at <strong>2.360×</strong>. In that measurement, larger is better. A speedup of 2 means the same defined work takes about half the baseline time.')+p('These are historical kernel results, not whole-server speedups. The public candidate passed five correctness cases and six measured shapes. Its uncertainty interval was about 2.325–2.395×. An uncertainty interval reminds us that measurements vary; tiny differences may not reliably repeat.')+note('A simple experiment loop','Predict what should improve. Change one thing. Check the answer. Measure fairly. Explain the result. Keep the improvement or restore the previous version.'))+
 exercise('A new version is faster at one token but much slower at 256 tokens. Can we call it the best?',p('Not without considering the target workload. We need all relevant shapes and their scoring or real-world importance. A narrow win can be outweighed by a large regression elsewhere.')),
 quiz:[{q:'Why run warmup before steady-state timing?',options:['To separate initial setup from repeated work','To skip correctness checks'],answer:0,why:'Compilation and initialization can make the first run unlike later serving calls.'},{q:'What does a profile help explain?',options:['Where time and resources are spent','Whether every future workload will be fast'],answer:0,why:'A profile describes observed execution. It cannot guarantee performance on unrelated workloads.'}]
},
serving:{
 title:'How a server handles many conversations',
 subtitle:'Fast arithmetic is one part of making users receive answers quickly.',
 goals:['Trace a request from arrival to generated output','Understand why batching and queues matter','Explain why more throughput can also mean longer waits'],
 body:h('What happens after a user sends a prompt?',p('The server receives text, turns it into tokens, waits for an opportunity to run it, processes the prompt, and starts generating tokens. It keeps the request’s history and sends output back to the user.')+flow(['Receive request','Make tokens','Wait if necessary','Prefill','Decode and stream','Finish'])+p('<strong>SGLang</strong> is a serving engine that coordinates this work. The model defines the calculations; the engine decides how requests, memory, and execution are managed. Improving one GPU kernel does not remove all the other steps.'))+
 h('Why run several requests together?',p('Imagine three conversations are waiting for their next token. The engine can group their token rows into one batch. The model can then reuse weights while processing those rows, instead of treating every request as a separate tiny job.')+p('But requests do not all start or end together. With <strong>continuous batching</strong>, the engine can change the active group between iterations: finished requests leave, and new work can join. It is like letting a free service slot accept the next customer rather than waiting for everyone in the original group to finish.'))+
 h('Why can a long prompt delay another user?',p('One user might send a short question while another sends a large document. Processing the document’s prompt can occupy the GPU for a while. Existing conversations still need their next generated token.')+p('<strong>Chunked prefill</strong> breaks prompt processing into smaller pieces so the scheduler can interleave work. That may improve responsiveness, but extra pieces also add overhead. The best choice depends on the actual requests and hardware.')+p('A <strong>token budget</strong> limits how many token rows are processed in an iteration. A <strong>memory budget</strong> limits how much state can stay on the GPU. One is a work limit; the other is a space limit.'))+
 h('More customers can raise throughput and waiting time',p('If ten requests each generate 30 output tokens per second, their combined output throughput is 300 tokens per second. That is <strong>aggregate</strong> throughput. Each user still sees about 30 tokens per second, not 300.')+p('Increasing concurrency—the number of requests active together—may improve weight reuse. Eventually memory, attention, or scheduling becomes a limit. Queues can grow, and a user can wait longer even when the server’s total output per second rises.')+p('You already know the basic metrics. The key practical rule is to check throughput together with first-token delay and later-token delays. <strong>Goodput</strong> counts useful work that still meets the chosen service targets.'))+
 h('How to make one useful serving experiment',p('Keep the model, GPU setup, prompt lengths, output lengths, and request arrival pattern fixed. Measure the baseline. Change one permitted setting or implementation. Then compare both throughput and delays, and inspect memory use.')+p('For the competition, use its pinned SGLang code and track rules. A change that is useful in general—such as a different numerical format—is not automatically allowed for that benchmark.')+note('The main idea','A serving engine is managing a moving queue of conversations, not repeatedly running one isolated matrix multiplication.'))+
 exercise('The server generates more tokens per second, but each user waits twice as long for the first token. Is it unconditionally better?',p('<strong>No.</strong> It depends on the service targets. A throughput improvement that violates the required latency may reduce goodput.')),
 quiz:[{q:'What does continuous batching allow?',options:['The active request group to change between iterations','All requests to have the same answer length'],answer:0,why:'Completed requests can leave and new work can join as the engine schedules iterations.'},{q:'Ten users each receive 30 tokens/s. The combined output throughput is…',options:['300 tokens/s','30 tokens/s'],answer:0,why:'Aggregate throughput sums the output across users: 10 × 30 = 300.'}]
},
distributed:{
 title:'When one GPU is not enough',
 subtitle:'Divide the model or the requests, then account for the cost of working together.',
 goals:['Distinguish four ways of using multiple GPUs','Explain why one busy GPU can delay the group','Understand draft-and-verify generation'],
 body:h('Why add more GPUs?',p('A model might be too large for one GPU, or one GPU might not serve enough requests. More GPUs can provide more storage and compute. But splitting a job creates a new cost: exchanging intermediate numbers and waiting for dependencies.')+p('Think about a group preparing a report. They can divide the research, but they must agree on the plan, exchange results, and combine their sections. More people do not guarantee that every stage becomes proportionally faster.'))+
 h('Four different ways to split the job',tbl(['Method','Plain-language idea','What must be considered'],[['Tensor parallelism','Split one large mathematical operation across GPUs','Combine partial results frequently'],['Expert parallelism','Put different experts on different GPUs','Route work and handle busy experts'],['Pipeline parallelism','Assign different layer ranges to different GPUs','Pass activations between stages'],['Data parallelism','Run model replicas for different requests','Each replica needs model storage']])+p('These approaches can be combined. The word “parallelism” just means organizing work so parts can proceed concurrently; the important question is which parts are split.'))+
 h('What does an all-reduce actually do?',p('A <strong>rank</strong> is one participant in a distributed group, commonly a GPU process. Suppose two ranks calculate partial output vectors:')+code('Rank 0 has: [1, 2]\nRank 1 has: [3, 4]\n\nSum the matching positions: [4, 6]\nGive the result to both ranks.', 'a sum all-reduce')+p('That operation is called <strong>all-reduce</strong>: combine contributions and give everyone the result. Other operations collect pieces or send different pieces to different participants. These communication patterns have both a startup delay and a cost for moving bytes.'))+
 h('Why does the busiest GPU matter?',p('Suppose seven GPUs finish their local work in 100 µs, while one finishes in 180 µs. If the next step needs every contribution, it must wait for that last GPU. Speeding one of the seven fast GPUs up to 90 µs might not change the step’s finishing time.')+p('This is particularly relevant to experts: popular experts can receive much more work. The Muse kernel challenge deliberately measures the busiest expert shard’s routing. A shard is the portion of the model or data owned by one participant.'))+
 h('Can a model generate several tokens at once?',p('Ordinary generation chooses the next token before choosing the one after it. <strong>Speculative decoding</strong> tries a different workflow: a cheaper draft process proposes several tokens, and the target model verifies them together.')+flow(['Draft proposes tokens','Target checks them','Accept valid continuation','Correct where needed'])+p('The target may reject some proposals. We care about accepted output tokens divided by the total draft-and-verification time, not simply how many proposals were generated. Correct methods use specific acceptance and correction rules; blindly trusting the draft would change the model’s output behavior.'))+
 h('What should you learn next?',p('Return to the small examples until you can explain them without the terminology. Then run the vector-add exercise, learn one reduction, and trace the simple expert MLP. After that, the public MoE kernel will have familiar building blocks instead of looking like one enormous mystery.')+note('Your working habit','Locate the slow part. Understand the numbers it reads and writes. Make one justified change. Check correctness. Measure the complete workload that matters.'))+
 exercise('Two GPUs take 40 ms and 70 ms, and the next step needs both. Does reducing the first GPU to 20 ms necessarily finish the step sooner?',p('<strong>No.</strong> It can still wait for the 70 ms GPU. The critical path—the chain of work determining completion time—has not necessarily changed.')),
 quiz:[{q:'Which method assigns different experts to different GPUs?',options:['Expert parallelism','Data parallelism'],answer:0,why:'Expert parallelism distributes experts. Data parallelism uses replicas for separate requests.'},{q:'Should rejected draft tokens count as output delivered to users?',options:['No','Yes'],answer:0,why:'They consumed compute but were not accepted as user-visible output.'}]
}
};

for (const chapter of chapters) {
 const lesson=simpleLessons[chapter.id];
 chapter.advancedBody=chapter.body;
 chapter.body=lesson.body;
 chapter.title=lesson.title;
 chapter.subtitle=lesson.subtitle;
 chapter.goals=lesson.goals;
 chapter.quiz=lesson.quiz.map((question,i)=>(chapters.indexOf(chapter)+i)%2?{...question,options:[...question.options].reverse(),answer:question.options.length-1-question.answer}:question);
 chapter.time=10;
}

glossary.push(
 ['Token','A piece of text processed by the model: a word, part of a word, punctuation, or another encoded unit.','tensors'],
 ['Vector','A list of numbers. A token’s representation is a vector with many coordinates.','tensors'],
 ['Matrix','A two-dimensional table of numbers, organized into rows and columns.','tensors'],
 ['Tensor','A container of numbers. A single number, a vector, and a matrix are all examples.','tensors'],
 ['Shape','The sizes of a tensor’s dimensions. A table with 2 rows and 3 columns has shape [2,3].','tensors'],
 ['Weight','A model weight is learned during training and normally fixed while serving. Attention and routing weights are different: they are calculated for the current inputs.','tensors'],
 ['Embedding','A learned initial vector associated with a token ID. Later layers change the representation using context.','tensors'],
 ['Bit / byte','A bit is one binary digit. Eight bits form a byte. More bytes per value usually mean more storage and data movement.','precision']
);

const labGuides={
 matmul:'Start with the row count: it means how many tokens we process together. Input width is the numbers each token starts with. Output width is the numbers we produce. Change only the row count first: the weight table stays the same, while the total arithmetic grows. “FLOPs” counts arithmetic operations; MiB measures storage.',
 kv:'Think of each request as a conversation with its own saved notes. First double the number of requests and watch the total memory double. Then change the context length: more saved positions need more space. Layers, heads, and head dimension describe how many key/value numbers the model keeps at each position. GiB is a unit of storage.',
 routing:'Each token chooses two of these four experts. Start with balanced routing, then make expert 0 popular. The total number of selected token–expert pairs stays the same, but the busiest expert gets more work. This tiny example uses top-2; Muse uses top-8.',
 roofline:'Compare two jobs: bringing in the numbers and doing the arithmetic. “Bytes moved” controls the first job; “compute work” controls the second. The bars show their idealized minimum times. Change one input and see which job becomes the larger limit. The result is an estimate, not a measured GPU time.',
 amdahl:'Imagine the original job takes 100 seconds. The first slider chooses how many of those seconds belong to the part you improve. The second chooses how much faster that part becomes. Watch how the unchanged part limits the total gain.',
 batch:'A request is one conversation. A token row is one position processed by the model. Ordinary decode contributes one row per request; verifying eight proposals contributes eight. Switch the operation and watch the row count change without changing the number of conversations.'
};
